    56	    large-sieve exceptional bound; the clean-band one-sidedness argument; rigour.
    57	  - We found no prior *proved* sub-1/5 almost-all result. Carella (arXiv 1308.2891) *claims*
    58	    deterministic 1/6; the r13 referee judges its proof incomplete (§10).
    59	
    60	## 1. Population, target and statement
    61	
    62	Throughout, n is the bit length, X = 2^n and P = 2^{n/2}.
    63	
    64	**Population.**
    65	
    66	  S_n = {(p, q) : p < q ≤ 2p primes, 2^{n−1} ≤ pq < 2^n}.
    67	
    68	It is counted by pairs, equivalently by semiprimes N = pq. Then p ∈ (P/2, P) and
    69	q ∈ (P/√2, √2 P). Counting with the PNT in [0.85X′, 0.9X′] × [1.2X′, 1.25X′],
    70	X′ = 2^{(n−1)/2}, gives |S_n| ≥ c₀ P²/n² with an absolute c₀ > 0 for n ≥ n₀.
    71	
    72	**Target.** A deterministic algorithm 𝒜_{n,ε} with the following properties.
    73	- It receives N (n bits) and outputs either a nontrivial divisor of N or FAIL.
    74	- Its worst-case cost on every n-bit input is N^{1/6+O(ε)} bit operations.
    75	- It outputs FAIL on at most O(N^{−ε})·|S_n| pairs of S_n.
    76	
    77	**Theorem D′ (proved; reviews in §7).** Fix ε ∈ (0, 1/60]. There are n₀(ε) and absolute
    78	constants C, c such that for n ≥ n₀ the algorithm DCL-det_{n,ε} of §2 has the following
    79	properties.
    80	- (i) **Correctness.** On every input it outputs a proper divisor d (1 < d < N, d | N) or
    81	  FAIL. Every candidate is validated before output (`algorithm._proper`).
    82	  - Inputs N < 4 return FAIL; even N ≥ 4 return 2; a band prime ℓ is returned only if
    83	    1 < ℓ < N.
    84	  - (Correction, r13: the pre-fix code returned 2 for N = 2 and ℓ for N = ℓ prime.)
    85	  - An exhaustive regression test covers all 2 ≤ N ≤ 10⁴ under two designs.
    86	- (ii) **Cost.** On every n-bit input it uses O(n^c·2^{(1/6+5ε/6)n}) bit operations, i.e.
    87	  N^{1/6+5ε/6}(log N)^{O(1)}.
    88	- (iii) **Success.** It outputs FAIL on at most C·2^{−εn}·|S_n| pairs (p,q) ∈ S_n.
    89	
    90	Equivalently: for every δ ∈ (0, 1/72], cost N^{1/6+δ+o(1)} with an exceptional fraction
    91	O(N^{−6δ/5}).
    92	
    93	**Corollary D″ (proved, same proof; polylog regime).** For every fixed a > 0, the same
    94	algorithm with H = ⌈n^{1+a}⌉ (and T = 2^{n/4}n^{−1−a} in the analysis) has cost
    95	N^{1/6}(log N)^{O(1+a)} and fails on O((log N)^{−2a}) of S_n.
    96	
    97	**Quantifier (audit item 5).**
    98	- Theorem D′ is a statement about the true factor of an input drawn from S_n, counted over
    99	  pairs.
   100	- It does **not** say that for almost every N all integer candidates p are covered, so it
   101	  does not show that the full-candidate covering model has exponent 1/6 for almost
   102	  every N.
   103	- It does not cover the worst case (§6), nor ratios q/p > 2.
   104	
   105	## 2. The algorithm DCL-det_{n,ε} (operational; `src/dcltheorem/algorithm.py`)
   106	
   107	### 2.1 Parameters
   108	
   109	All parameters are functions of n and ε only; nothing depends on N.
   110	
   111	- H = ⌈n·2^{εn/2}⌉. The code (`params.theorem_design`) uses n·⌊2^{εn/2}⌋ and ⌊H^{1/3}⌋ in Y. These are constant-factor conventions, which the proof allows.
   112	- V = {(A,B) ∈ [1,H]² : gcd(A,B) = 1, A ≠ B}. Then H²/2 ≤ |V| ≤ H² for large H.
   113	- Y = ⌈2^{n/6}H^{−1/3}⌉.
   114	- For k = 0, 1, 2, 3: 𝒫_k = {ℓ prime : ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4), 2^kY < ℓ ≤ 2^{k+1}Y} and
   115	  L_k = ∏_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} ℓ².
   116	- m̄_k = ⌊span̄/(2^kY)²⌋ + 2, with span̄ = ⌊(5/2)·H·(2^{n/2}+1)⌋ + 2H + 3. This is an upper
   117	  bound for every window width in block k (§2.2).
   118	- The base is β = 2.
   119	
   120	### 2.2 Steps
   121	
   122	Input N (n bits). If N is even, output 2. Set a₀ = ⌈√(N/2)⌉ and b₀ = ⌊√N⌋. Every (p,q) ∈ S_n
   123	has p ∈ [a₀, b₀].
   124	
   125	For v = (A,B) ∈ V put:
   126	- Lo_v = ⌊2√(ABN)⌋. By AM–GM, Lo_v ≤ F_v(x) := Bx + AN/x for all x > 0.
   127	- Hi_v = max_{x∈{a₀,b₀}} ⌈F_v(x)⌉. By convexity, F_v ≤ Hi_v on [a₀, b₀].
   128	- Then Hi_v − Lo_v ≤ (B + √2A)√N + 2H + 2 ≤ span̄.
   129	
   130	For each block k = 0, 1, 2, 3:
   131	
   132	1. **Exponent tree.**
   133	   - Build the balanced integer product tree of {ℓ² : ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k}.
   134	   - Push 2 down the tree: a node holding 2^{L/∏node} hands 2^{L/∏node·∏sibling} to each
   135	     child. This yields u_ℓ = 2^{L_k/ℓ²} mod N for all ℓ.
   136	   - Set γ = u_{ℓ₀}^{ℓ₀²} = 2^{L_k} mod N.
   137	   - If ℓ | N for some ℓ, output ℓ.
   138	2. **Targets.** For ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k and v ∈ V:
   139	   - a := A N B^{−1} mod ℓ. If a^{(ℓ−1)/2} ≢ 1 (mod ℓ), skip.
   140	   - Otherwise r₀ = a^{(ℓ+1)/4} mod ℓ, and the roots of Br² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) are {r₀, ℓ−r₀}.
   141	   - For each root r:
   142	     - s = N r^{−1} mod ℓ² and c = (Br + As) mod ℓ²;
   143	     - g_lo = ⌈(Lo_v − c)/ℓ²⌉ ≥ 0 and g_hi = ⌊(Hi_v − c)/ℓ²⌋;
   144	     - W_τ = g_hi − g_lo + 1 ≤ span̄/ℓ² + 1 ≤ m̄_k.
   145	   - The target is τ = (v, ℓ, r). Write T_k for the number of targets: T_k ≤ 2|V||𝒫_k|.
   146	3. **Baby length.** Either m = m̄_k (the theorem's default), or the implementation's
   147	   *endpoint-competitive* rule: minimise m + Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m⌉ over an integer geometric grid
   148	   that ends at W_max = max_τ W_τ ≤ m̄_k.
   149	   - The success proof needs only m ≤ m̄_k.
   150	   - The **cost** proof needs one of these two rules. Under either, m + T′ ≤ m̄_k + T_k,
   151	     with T′ = Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m⌉.
   152	   - *Correction (GPT-6.1-sol dclt_1).* An earlier draft said "the proof needs only
   153	     m ≤ m̄_k". For cost that is false: m = 1 on prime-square inputs gives
   154	     T′ ≫ H²P/(Y log Y).
   155	4. **Giants.**
   156	   - Precompute w_ℓ = u_ℓ^N for each ℓ.
   157	   - For each target and 0 ≤ j < ⌈W_τ/m⌉:
   158	
   159	       G_{τ,j} = w_ℓ^A · u_ℓ^{B−c+ℓ²} · γ^{−(g_lo+1)} · γ^{−jm}  ≡  u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}·γ^{−(g_lo+jm)}  (mod N).
   160	
   161	     The congruence holds because u_ℓ^{ℓ²} = γ. γ^{−1} exists because gcd(2,N) = 1.
   162	5. **Collision detection.**
   163	   - f(X) = ∏_{0≤i<m}(X − γ^i) ∈ (Z/N)[X], built by a product tree.
   164	   - f(G_{τ,j}) for all giants, by a remainder tree. Only monic divisors are used, so this
   165	     is valid over Z/N.
   166	   - For each giant, d = gcd(f(G), N). If 1 < d < N, output d.
   167	
   168	Output FAIL if no block yields a divisor.
   169	
   170	**Remarks.**
   171	- (a) No order is computed, assumed or certified. No randomness is used.
   172	- (b) The symmetric weight (1,1) is excluded. For A = B the true giant always collides mod
   173	  q as well (Lemma 4 with A − B = 0), so it can never be one-sided.
   174	- (c) The four bands are needed only for Lemma 6, which guarantees a "clean" band.
   175	
   176	## 3. Lemmas
   177	
   178	Fix (p,q) ∈ S_n, N = pq, a block k, and write L = L_k, u = u_ℓ and d := ord_q(2).
   179	
   180	**Lemma 1 (class lock; HH 2022 Prop. 3.3).** Let ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k, (A,B) ∈ V and r a root, and
   181	suppose p ≡ r (mod ℓ). Then g := (Bp + Aq − c)/ℓ² ∈ Z.
   182	
   183	*Proof.*
   184	- ℓ > Y > H ≥ A, B, and ℓ < p, so ℓ ∤ ABN. Write p = r + ℓx.
   185	- Mod ℓ²: p^{−1} ≡ r^{−1}(1 − ℓxr^{−1}), so q = Np^{−1} ≡ s − ℓx·s r^{−1}.
   186	- Hence Bp + Aq ≡ c + ℓx(B − A s r^{−1}).
   187	- s r^{−1} ≡ N r^{−2} (mod ℓ) and Br² ≡ AN, so B − ANr^{−2} ≡ 0 (mod ℓ). The last term is
   188	  therefore ≡ 0 (mod ℓ²). ∎
   189	
   190	**Coverage criterion.** p mod ℓ is one of the computed roots ⇔ Bp² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) ⇔
   191	ℓ | Bp − Aq (cancel p, since ℓ ∤ p). We say τ = (v, ℓ, p mod ℓ) *covers* p.
   192	
   193	**Lemma 2 (window).** If τ covers p, then g_lo ≤ g ≤ g_hi. Put i_full = g − g_lo,
   194	j* = ⌊i_full/m⌋ and i* = i_full − j*m ∈ [0, m).
   195	
   196	*Proof.* p ∈ [a₀, b₀], so Lo_v ≤ F_v(p) = Bp + Aq ≤ Hi_v. ∎
   197	
   198	**Lemma 3 (mod p).** If τ covers p, then G_{τ,j*} ≡ γ^{i*} (mod p). No hypothesis on any
   199	order is needed.
   200	
   201	*Proof.*
   202	- B + AN − (Bp + Aq) = (p − 1)(Aq − B), and 2^{p−1} ≡ 1 (mod p).
   203	- So u^{B+AN−c} ≡ 2^{(L/ℓ²)(Bp+Aq−c)} = 2^{Lg} = γ^g (mod p).
   204	- Multiply by γ^{−(g_lo + j*m)}. ∎
   205	
   206	**Lemma 4 (mod q).** If τ covers p, then G_{τ,j*} ≡ γ^{i*}·u^{(A−B)(p−1)} (mod q).
   207	
   208	*Proof.*
   209	- Mod q − 1: N ≡ p, so B + AN − c ≡ B + Ap − c.
   210	- Also ℓ²g = Bp + Aq − c ≡ Bp + A − c.
   211	- The difference is (A − B)(p − 1). Multiplying by L/ℓ² preserves the congruence mod q − 1,
   212	  and 2^{q−1} ≡ 1 (mod q). ∎
   213	
   214	**Lemma 5 (one-sided extraction).** Call the true giant G = G_{τ,j*} *FULL* if
   215	G ≡ γ^{i′} (mod q) for some i′ ∈ [0, m). If τ covers p and G is not FULL, then
   216	gcd(f(G), N) = p.
   217	
   218	*Proof.*
   219	- f(G) = ∏_i (G − γ^i).
   220	- The factor i = i* vanishes mod p (Lemma 3).
   221	- Mod q, no factor vanishes, by definition, and Z/q is a field.
   222	- So p | f(G) and q ∤ f(G). ∎
   223	
   224	**Lemma 6 (clean band).** Call block k *clean for q* if no ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k divides q − 1. If
   225	Y⁴ > √2P, at least one of the four blocks is clean for q.
   226	
   227	*Proof.* Every band prime exceeds Y. Four distinct band primes dividing q − 1 would give
   228	q − 1 > Y⁴ > √2P > q. So at most 3 blocks contain a prime factor of q − 1. ∎
   229	
   230	Asymptotically: Y⁴ ≥ 2^{2n/3}H^{−4/3}, and 2^{n/6} > √2 H^{4/3} = √2 n^{4/3}2^{2εn/3}
   231	for ε < 1/4 and n large.
   232	
   233	**Lemma 7 (FULL in a clean block).** If block k is clean for q, then the true giant is FULL
   234	iff d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ for some δ ∈ [−i*, m−1−i*] ⊂ (−m, m).
   235	
   236	*Proof.*
   237	- By Lemma 4, FULL ⇔ u^{(A−B)(p−1)} ≡ γ^{δ} (mod q) with δ = i′ − i*.
   238	- Equivalently 2^{(L/ℓ²)[(A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ]} ≡ 1 (mod q).
   239	- Since d | q − 1 and gcd(L, q − 1) = 1, gcd(d, L/ℓ²) = 1. ∎
   240	
   241	**Lemma 8 (small orders are rare; elementary).** For Q₀ ≥ 2 and T ≥ 1,
   242	
   243	  #{q > Q₀ prime : ord_q(2) ≤ T} ≤ T(T+1)/(2 log₂ Q₀).
   244	
   245	*Proof.* Such a q divides 2^k − 1 for some k ≤ T. Since 2^k − 1 < 2^k, it has fewer than
   246	k/log₂Q₀ prime factors > Q₀. Sum over k ≤ T. ∎
   247	
   248	**Lemma 9 (coverage: the arithmetic large sieve, in the audit's repaired form).** Fix q and
   249	k. Let U_k(q) be the set of integers p ∈ [P/2, P] that no target of block k covers, i.e.
   250	ℓ ∤ Bp − Aq for all (v, ℓ) ∈ V × 𝒫_k. For n ≥ n₀,
   251	
   252	  |U_k(q)| ≤ (P/2 + 1 + 4^{k+1}Y²)/J_k,  J_k ≥ |V|·Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ ≥ c₁H²/n.
   253	
   254	*Proof.*
   255	- For ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k, ℓ ∤ q, so ℓ | Bp − Aq ⇔ p ≡ AqB^{−1} (mod ℓ).
   256	- These |V| classes are distinct: AqB^{−1} ≡ A′qB′^{−1} implies ℓ | AB′ − A′B, and
   257	  0 < |AB′ − A′B| < H² < Y < ℓ. This uses Y > H², i.e. 2^{n/6} > n^{7/3}2^{7εn/6}, true for
   258	  ε < 1/7 and n large.
   259	- So U_k(q) avoids ω(ℓ) = |V| < ℓ classes mod every ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k.
   260	- The arithmetic large sieve (Montgomery 1968; Montgomery–Vaughan 1973, constant
   261	  N₀ + Q² for an interval of N₀ integers) with Q = 2^{k+1}Y gives
   262	  |U| ≤ (N₀ + Q²)/J, J = Σ_{d≤Q} μ²(d)∏_{ℓ|d} ω(ℓ)/(ℓ − ω(ℓ)).
   263	- Only the prime terms are needed, and they follow directly. For each ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k,
   264	  Parseval and Cauchy–Schwarz give Σ_{a=1}^{ℓ−1}|S(a/ℓ)|² ≥ ω/(ℓ−ω)·|U|². The fractions
   265	  a/ℓ are Q^{−2}-separated, so the additive large sieve applies (referee dclt_1).
   266	- Keeping only d = 1 and d = ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k: J ≥ |V| Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ.
   267	- By the PNT in the progression 3 mod 4 (effective for the fixed modulus 4),
   268	  Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} 1/ℓ ≥ (1/2 − o(1))·ln 2/ln(2^{k+1}Y) ≥ c′/n.
   269	- |V| ≥ H²/2 for large H, since the coprime pairs number (6/π² + o(1))H². ∎
   270	
   271	**Lemma 10 (FULL is rare).** Fix q with d = ord_q(2) > T and a block k clean for q. Let
   272	F_k(q) be the set of integers p ∈ [P/2, P] such that some (v, ℓ) ∈ V × 𝒫_k has ℓ | Bp − Aq
   273	and d | (A−B)(p−1) − ℓ²δ for some |δ| < m̄_k. Then
   274	
   275	  |F_k(q)| ≤ Σ_{v,ℓ,|δ|<m̄_k} (P·H/(2·2^kY·T) + 1)
   276	           ≤ 2|V||𝒫_k| m̄_k·(PH/(2^{k+1}YT) + 1).
   277	
   278	*Proof.*
   279	- Fix (v, ℓ, δ) and let g′ = gcd(A − B, d) ≤ H (A ≠ B, |A − B| < H).
   280	- The congruence (A−B)(p−1) ≡ ℓ²δ (mod d) is either unsolvable, or holds exactly on one
   281	  class of p mod d/g′.
   282	- ℓ ∤ d, because clean means ℓ ∤ q − 1. With p ≡ AqB^{−1} (mod ℓ), CRT gives one class mod
   283	  ℓd/g′ ≥ ℓd/H.
   284	- An interval of length P/2 contains ≤ P H/(2ℓd) + 1 such integers. ∎
   285	
   286	## 4. Proof of Theorem D′
   287	
   288	**(i) Correctness.** Only gcds with N in (1, N), or band primes dividing N, are output.
   289	
   290	**(ii) Cost (bit operations; M(b) = O(b log b) multiplication; per block, with T_k
   291	targets and m ≤ m̄_k).**
   292	
   293	| step | operations | bit cost |
   294	|---|---|---|
   295	| prime sieve to 16Y, V, Lo_v/Hi_v (isqrt of O(n)-bit integers) | O(Y log log Y) + O(H²) isqrt | Õ(Y + H²n) |
   296	| integer product tree of ℓ² (Σ bits = log₂L_k ≤ 3·2^kY) | balanced tree | O(M(Y)·log Y) |
   297	| exponent tree (per level Σ exponent bits ≤ log₂L_k + #nodes; ⌈log₂|𝒫_k|⌉ levels) + γ, γ^{−1} | O(Y log Y) mults mod N + 1 inversion | O(Y log Y·M(n)) |
   298	| roots (Euler test, powering mod ℓ), s, c, windows | O(\|V\|\|𝒫_k\| log Y) mults mod ℓ, ℓ² | Õ(H²Y) |
   299	| w_ℓ = u_ℓ^N; giants (exponents of O(n) bits) | O(n\|𝒫_k\| + nT_k + T′) mults mod N | O((n(T_k + Y) + T′)·M(n)) |
   300	| choice of m (endpoint-competitive grid: O(n) grid points × T_k targets); inversions B^{−1} mod ℓ and r^{−1} mod ℓ²; reductions N mod ℓ, ℓ² | O(nT_k) word/small-integer ops | Õ(T_k n^{O(1)}) |
   301	| babies γ^i | m mults | O(m M(n)) |
   302	| f by product tree; remainder tree at the T′ = Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m⌉ ≤ T_k + Σ_τ W_τ/m giants | O((M(m) + M(T′)) log(m+T′)) ring ops; Kronecker substitution makes a degree-D product O(M(D·(2n + log D))) bits | Õ((m + T′)n) |
   303	| gcds | T′ gcds of n-bit numbers | O(T′ M(n) log n) |
   304	
   305	With m = m̄_k (or any m that minimises the list count) the list is
   306	m + T′ ≤ m̄_k + T_k + 1 ≤ 2.5HP/Y² + 4 + 2H²·2^{k+1}Y. Over the four blocks:
   307	
   308	  total = O(n^{O(1)}·(HP/Y² + H²Y)) = O(n^{O(1)} H^{5/3} P^{1/3}),
   309	
   310	because Y ≍ P^{1/3}H^{−1/3}. With H = n·2^{εn/2} this is O(n^{O(1)}·2^{(1/6+5ε/6)n}).
   311	
   312	**Arithmetic model (referee dclt_1, item 4).**
   313	- The bound assumes quasi-linear integer multiplication M(b) = O(b log b) (Harvey–van der
   314	  Hoeven; Schönhage–Strassen gives an extra log log). This applies to the exponent product
   315	  tree, the Kronecker-substituted polynomial products and the gcds.
   316	- Kronecker substitution over composite N: coefficients lie in [0, N), and the slots are
   317	  wider than 2n + ⌈log₂(D+1)⌉.
   318	- Monic remainders: reversal plus Newton inversion of a power series with constant term 1.
   319	  No inverse of a nonunit is ever needed.
   320	- The experimental metric E (§8) is a *proxy*. It is not the proof of (ii). The Python
   321	  implementation uses GMP integer products (gmpy2) and flint polynomial arithmetic. Its
   322	  `powN` charges binary square-and-multiply counts.
   323	
   324	**(iii) Exceptional set.**
   325	- Let T = ⌊2^{(1/4−ε)n}⌋. For (p,q) ∈ S_n, let k(q) be the least block clean for q
   326	  (Lemma 6). If
   327	  - (a) d = ord_q(2) > T,
   328	  - (b) p ∉ U_{k(q)}(q), and
   329	  - (c) p ∉ F_{k(q)}(q),
   330	
   331	  then DCL-det succeeds.
   332	- Indeed by (b) some target τ of block k(q) covers p (Lemma 1, coverage criterion).
   333	- By (c) and Lemma 7, its true giant is not FULL: the exact δ-range lies inside |δ| < m̄_k.
   334	- By Lemma 5 that giant's gcd is p. The algorithm outputs a nontrivial divisor at block k(q)
   335	  at the latest. It may output q, or output earlier from another block; both are fine.
   336	- So the FAIL pairs lie in E_a ∪ E_b ∪ E_c, with E_a = {ord_q(2) ≤ T},
   337	  E_b = {d > T, p ∈ U_{k(q)}(q)} and E_c = {d > T, p ∈ F_{k(q)}(q)}.
   338	
   339	The counts sum over prime q ∈ (P/√2, √2P), of which there are ≤ 3P/n for n large:
   340	
   341	- **|E_a|.** By Lemma 8 with Q₀ = 2^{(n−1)/2}, each bad q has ≤ P/2 + 1 partners, so
   342	  |E_a| ≤ (T(T+1)/(n−1))·(P/2+1) ≤ 2^{(1−2ε)n}/n·(1 + o(1)). Then
   343	  |E_a|/|S_n| ≤ (n/c₀)·2^{−2εn}(1 + o(1)).
   344	  - *Correction (dclt_1):* T(T+1) replaces T². The estimate is unchanged.
   345	- **|E_b|.** By Lemma 9, Σ_q |U_{k(q)}(q)| ≤ (3P/n)·(P/2 + 1 + 256Y²)·n/(c₁H²) ≤ C P²/H².
   346	  So |E_b|/|S_n| ≤ C n²/H² ≤ C 2^{−εn}. This is the audit's repaired normalisation: one
   347	  fixed interval, integers counted, no short-interval prime counts.
   348	- **|E_c|.** By Lemma 10 with |V| ≤ H², |𝒫_k| ≤ 2^kY and m̄_k ≤ 3HP/(4^kY²):
   349	  |F_k(q)| ≤ 6H⁴P²/(Y²T) + 6H³P/Y. So
   350	
   351	    |E_c|/|S_n| ≤ (3P/n)(6H⁴P²/(Y²T) + 6H³P/Y)·n²/(c₀P²) ≤ C n (H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T + H^{10/3}/P^{1/3}),
   352	
   353	  using Y² ≥ P^{2/3}H^{−2/3}. Here H^{14/3}P^{1/3}/T ≤ (2n)^{14/3}2^{(−1/12 + 7ε/3 + ε)n} and
   354	  H^{10/3}/P^{1/3} ≤ n^{4}2^{(5ε/3 − 1/6)n}.
